A||||13||gmata||Replacement of Factory Stereo||||5||
Z||000000||gmata||06-02-2000||01:17 PM||||I would like to replace my Factory Stereo.  Since I can get GM parts at wholesale prices, I was wondering what GM Stereo would fit a Fiero?  ie.(Firebird or Grand AM Stereo)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-02-2000||01:21 PM||||My 89 Bonneville, 86 Calais, and 94 GMC Safari all have the same size radio as my 85 Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lennyb4||06-02-2000||01:24 PM||||Basicly any Din sized radio should fit. Mine is out of a 90 Berretta GTZ. All late mode radios mount almost identically the same way.||||reg||1
Z||000003||contra||06-02-2000||01:32 PM||||pretty much any stereo can fit in the fiero wether it's full size like the original or the new slim size like most aftermarket ones or cd players. If you want to go aftermarket like my kenwood system, you just buy a spacer for a couple bucks at the local stereo shop, and that way you can just slip it onto your cd player or what not and just bolt the whole thing into the cavity where the old radio went.<BR>just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||06-02-2000||01:35 PM||||here is a nice one -- out of a 1999 or 2000 model Pontiac....... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gmplayer.jpg">  -- it's a DELCO GM PONTIAC AM /FM CD WITH A 7-BAND EQUALIZER||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-02-2000||02:04 PM||||The size in the Plastic Fantastic (and most other GM vehicles) is 1.5 DIN (or DIN and a half).  The size of most aftermarket stereos is just DIN.  If you go with an aftermarket stereo (not just a different GM stereo) you'll either need a mounting kit (about $15 almost anywhere from Autozone to Wally World) or need to custom fabricate your own mount.  I just got done fabbing my own out of a piece of aluminum.  VERY rough right now.  It needs tons of refinements, but it's a start.  If you do this you either need to add spacers or bend your mounting tabs in.  Otherwise you get a sizable gap between the mounting plate and the trim plate (just discovered this a little while ago <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000006||gmata||06-02-2000||02:10 PM||||The pontiac delco stereo is exactly what I'm looking for.  If the price is right, I'm going to buy it.  If not, I planning on buying the Pioneer DEHP77DH.  It should fit the Fiero without a kit.<P>Thanks again.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Raydar||06-02-2000||02:15 PM||||I know that you said you want a GM piece, but there are some aftermarket radios that are designeed to fit in a GM (or Chrysler) dash opening. <BR>Pioneer makes two different ones.<BR>Alpine makes two different ones. <BR>Jensen make several, including one that has both a cassette and CD in the same unit. Prices are comparable to GM units. ($250-350)||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||06-02-2000||03:30 PM||||Hey tgowens, how do you disable the Theftlock feature in order to use the newer Pontiac stereo in a Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||06-02-2000||03:38 PM||||well ChadMan, unless you can get the local GM dealer to cooperate with you, you can't! GM really doesn't care if these radios are sold out of junk cars or not -- what you need to do is find one from the original owner and have them disable the Theftlock feature OR find one elsewhere that has the Theftlock feature disabled -- other than that, it's a pain in the Theftlock!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||06-02-2000||03:58 PM||||Raydar--You have a good point, but alot of it depends on what you want to do with your stereo.  If you want to use it for the beginnings of a complete system, then some of those you mentioned are probably not the way to go.  One of the reasons is because some of them only have one pre-out.  I don't know alot about stereos, but if you only have one pre-out to an amp, you can't fade front to rear.  On the other hand, if you just want to upgrade and don't want an amp,etc, some of those you mentioned are an excellent way to go.  Just my 2 pennies.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||06-03-2000||12:25 PM||||Monkeyman, you're right, especially regarding the Jensen units. <BR>I believe that all but the Jensens have at least two sets of pre-outs.  <BR>At least one of the Pioneer units has three sets of pre-outs. Two of them fade, and the third is fixed. The third one also has a switchable low pass filter, so you can go straight to a sub amp without having to mess with a crossover. <BR>Most of these head units, with the possible exception of the Jensen, probably have enough power (40-45 watts RMS, per speaker) to make your ears ring, without using any extra amps, except for the sub. <P>The Alpine units are at: <A HREF="http://www.alpine1.com/html/De_n_150_n_n.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.alpine1.com/html/De_n_150_n_n.html</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.alpine1.com/html/De_n_149_n_n.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.alpine1.com/html/De_n_149_n_n.html</A> <BR> The second Alpine unit (and maybe the first one, as well) has switchable illumination.<P>The others are at <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com</A> <P>So what am I gonna put in mine?<BR>A Delco CD with EQ, for a 94 Grand Am.<BR>AATechnologies has 'em for $240.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||06-03-2000||02:52 PM||||I was thinking about the Jensens.  I wouldn't say that all Jensens are cheap radios, but most (if not all) of them do seem to be low end.  I have a JVC head unit (cassette).  It only has 2 pre outs.  I like the unit (green illum only <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">) but I was still thinking of switching to something with a 3rd pre out.  I've seen some seriously cool stereos, but I just can't justify the money.  I want a great system, but it doesn't have to be competitive.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Raydar||06-03-2000||11:01 PM||||Monkeyman,<BR>JVC makes some nice equipment. Several years ago they made one (a cassette) that was a direct fit for GM cars. The model# is KS-RX710.<BR>Even had amber illumination. Bought one for the former Firebird. <BR>Had NO pre-outs. (I fixed that, with a bit of help from the service manual.)<BR>Had line-inputs! Controlled by a button on the front panel. Was designed for portable CD players and such. Wired my (usta be FM) Kenwood CD changer into the line-ins. Still use the FM controller, just disabled the FM modulator part. Actually worked! <BR>This whole mess is installed in my truck, now. Have an XM-5 crossover, three amps, and two twelves in an isobaric cabinet. <BR>It's starting to get a bit "ragged around the edges". Needs to be gone through. But the pain still sets in before the distortion.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
